Anna Chen: Quick follow-up from the ABI breakout RFC thread — we need a concrete compat/migration plan before landing the change that adds a `ResidencyToken` to alloc() :thinking:

Mike O'Neill: Agree. Can't change the signature in one jump. Proposal: keep old alloc(sz) but add an optional token out-param via metadata map. New callers can opt-in with `enable_residency_tokens`. Quick sketch:
```c++
// old
AllocResult alloc(size_t sz);
// internal shim keeps mapping when feature flag set
AllocResult alloc(size_t sz) {
  auto res = alloc_impl(sz);
  if (flags.enable_residency_tokens)
    metadata_map.emplace(res.id, generate_token());
  return res;
}
// new callers (opt-in)
AllocResult alloc(size_t sz, ResidencyToken *out_tok);
```
This avoids an immediate ABI break while letting us iterate.

Raj Patel: Mapping is fine for long-lived allocations but will blow up for tiny/stub allocations (we saw that in previous telemetry). Need to compare memory overhead of:
- sidecar unordered_map<alloc_id, token>
- per-allocation header in host-backed slab (compact header 8 bytes)

Sasha Ivanov: If we pack a header into host-backed pages, eviction and hot-score need to be aware. Right now hot-score looks at user-visible bytes only. We should either: adjust hot-score to include header residency, or store header in a separate compact slab to avoid skewing hotset. Tradeoffs: co-locating header = faster lookup, separate slab = less eviction noise.

Lina Gomez: From product/security POV: customers require no perf regressions and deterministic rollout. Suggestion: feature-flagged rollout path:
- Stage 0: CI/bench + unit tests (must pass)
- Stage 1: internal Dedicated canary (5% capacity) for 48h
- Stage 2: increase to 25% if error/latency metrics OK
- Auto-fallback: if hostpool mem delta > 0.5% or tail latency + p95 > baseline + 10%, revert on the fly
Also need a migration doc for Private customers (on-prem) — can't rely on server-side shim there.

alloc-bot: CI alert: `alloc-header-proto` job on x86-staging shows +0.8% peak host memory and one flaky test (hostpage-inflight-timeout). https://ci.internal/redwood/alloc-header-proto/1234 :warning:

Mike O'Neill: Ok noted. For Private we should provide a small compatibility shim library (hostpool-compat.so) that pins old symbol and implements the mapping locally. That will let on-prem users upgrade without recompiling callers. But it adds complexity to packaging.

Raj Patel: I'll run a quick microbenchmark comparing map vs header. Hypothesis: header approach uses ~8B extra per alloc (plus alignment) but map has ~32B overhead per entry. For workloads with many tiny allocations (token caches, small tensors) header wins. For large allocs mapping overhead is negligible relative to payload.

Sasha Ivanov: Additional consideration: we must ensure header layout is stable across kernel ABI and quantization code paths. If header placement changes, memory offset assumptions in eviction can break. Add unit tests for header alignment and a sentinel pattern for scanning.

Anna Chen: Action items:
- Mike: prototype shim + compat library sketch (EOD)
- Raj: microbench numbers (map vs header) + mem overhead estimate (tomorrow AM)
- Sasha: draft hot-score adjustments and header-scan unit tests (tomorrow)
- Lina: rollout + rollback doc + private packaging notes (tomorrow)

Anna Chen: Also please add a small config snippet for runtime flags we'll use during canary: 
```json
{
  "hostpool": {
    "enable_residency_tokens": true,
    "residency_mode": "header", // or "map"
    "canary_percent": 5
  }
}
```

Mike O'Neill: I'll post a prototype branch link when done. If alloc-bot CI keeps showing >0.5% memory, don't promote. :thumbsup:

Lina Gomez: tagging @security for packaging notes for private customers, will loop them in the doc.

Anna Chen: Thanks everyone — we'll reconvene here once Raj/Mike have numbers/branch.
